WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.673
Talking to Your Manager.

2
00:00:01.953 --> 00:00:06.049
Day 62 of the Soft Skill Playbook course, in the Workplace phase.

3
00:00:07.799 --> 00:00:08.823
The key idea.

4
00:00:09.103 --> 00:00:15.012
Your manager's three wishes: no surprises, clear status, and problems that arrive with options.

5
00:00:15.292 --> 00:00:17.660
Give them those three things and they'll trust you.

6
00:00:19.010 --> 00:00:19.863
Part 1.

7
00:00:20.143 --> 00:00:21.999
What your manager actually needs.

8
00:00:23.249 --> 00:00:24.593
Here is a quick table:

9
00:00:24.873 --> 00:00:26.516
They need, and So you should.

10
00:00:26.796 --> 00:00:27.884
Predictability:

11
00:00:28.164 --> 00:00:29.871
Share status before they ask.

12
00:00:30.151 --> 00:00:31.153
Early warning:

13
00:00:31.433 --> 00:00:34.676
Raise risks the moment you see them, not on deadline day.

14
00:00:34.956 --> 00:00:36.684
Options, not just problems:

15
00:00:36.964 --> 00:00:39.652
Bring 2 possible solutions with every problem.

16
00:00:39.932 --> 00:00:41.383
Something to tell their boss:

17
00:00:41.663 --> 00:00:44.756
Summarise in 1 to 2 sentences they can forward.

18
00:00:45.036 --> 00:00:46.188
To know you're growing:

19
00:00:46.468 --> 00:00:48.644
Talk about goals and ask for feedback.

20
00:00:49.994 --> 00:00:50.869
Part 2.

21
00:00:51.149 --> 00:00:52.514
The one-on-one meeting.

22
00:00:53.764 --> 00:00:56.175
A simple agenda (send it the day before).

23
00:00:56.455 --> 00:00:59.911
Here is a template you can copy. one-on-one agenda, Date.

24
00:01:00.191 --> 00:01:00.831
1.

25
00:01:01.111 --> 00:01:03.521
Wins this week: shipped X, fixed Y.

26
00:01:03.801 --> 00:01:04.505
2.

27
00:01:04.785 --> 00:01:06.151
In progress or risks:

28
00:01:06.431 --> 00:01:07.924
Z is at risk because...

29
00:01:08.204 --> 00:01:08.951
3.

30
00:01:09.231 --> 00:01:12.431
Need from you: decision on A or intro to team B.

31
00:01:12.711 --> 00:01:13.415
4.

32
00:01:13.695 --> 00:01:15.892
Growth: feedback on my design review?

33
00:01:18.742 --> 00:01:19.382
Tip:

34
00:01:19.662 --> 00:01:21.262
The one-on-one belongs to you.

35
00:01:21.542 --> 00:01:23.846
Don't let it turn into another status meeting.

36
00:01:24.126 --> 00:01:26.878
Keep 10 minutes for growth, feedback, and career.

37
00:01:28.228 --> 00:01:29.124
Part 3.

38
00:01:29.404 --> 00:01:31.260
Scripts for common situations.

39
00:01:32.510 --> 00:01:33.982
Giving a status update.

40
00:01:34.262 --> 00:01:35.201
You could say:

41
00:01:35.481 --> 00:01:38.894
Quick update on the payments API: on track for Friday.

42
00:01:39.174 --> 00:01:41.947
Endpoints are done, tests are 80% done.

43
00:01:42.227 --> 00:01:45.705
One risk: we're still waiting on the bank sandbox credentials.

44
00:01:45.985 --> 00:01:48.609
If they don't arrive by Wednesday, we slip a day.

45
00:01:48.889 --> 00:01:50.446
I've followed up with their team.

46
00:01:51.796 --> 00:01:52.905
Asking for help.

47
00:01:53.185 --> 00:01:54.124
You could say:

48
00:01:54.404 --> 00:01:57.881
I've been stuck on the Kafka consumer lag for about 4 hours.

49
00:01:58.161 --> 00:02:01.511
I've tried increasing partitions and tuning the batch size.

50
00:02:01.791 --> 00:02:05.119
Could you suggest someone who's worked on this, or spare 15 minutes?

51
00:02:06.469 --> 00:02:07.215
Note:

52
00:02:07.495 --> 00:02:09.245
The 2-hour rule (beginners).

53
00:02:09.525 --> 00:02:13.685
Struggle alone for up to ~2 hours, then ask, with what you tried.

54
00:02:13.965 --> 00:02:16.610
Asking too late costs more than asking too early.

55
00:02:17.960 --> 00:02:19.581
Raising a risk or delay.

56
00:02:19.861 --> 00:02:20.800
You could say:

57
00:02:21.080 --> 00:02:22.723
I want to flag a risk early.

58
00:02:23.003 --> 00:02:25.349
The search feature may slip by about 1 week.

59
00:02:25.629 --> 00:02:29.213
The OpenSearch mapping needs a reindex we didn't plan for.

60
00:02:29.493 --> 00:02:30.475
Two options:

61
00:02:30.755 --> 00:02:33.891
A) Ship on time without fuzzy search, add it next sprint.

62
00:02:34.171 --> 00:02:36.517
B) Keep full scope, ship next Friday.

63
00:02:36.797 --> 00:02:37.992
I'd recommend A.

64
00:02:38.272 --> 00:02:39.467
Which would you prefer?

65
00:02:40.817 --> 00:02:42.331
Pushing back on a deadline.

66
00:02:42.611 --> 00:02:43.550
You could say:

67
00:02:43.830 --> 00:02:46.881
I want to hit this date, so let me be realistic about it.

68
00:02:47.161 --> 00:02:50.297
With the current scope, my estimate is 3 weeks, not 2.

69
00:02:50.577 --> 00:02:55.761
To make 2 weeks, we could drop the export feature, or add one more developer for the UI.

70
00:02:56.041 --> 00:02:57.790
What matters most for this release?

71
00:02:59.140 --> 00:03:00.356
Admitting a mistake.

72
00:03:00.636 --> 00:03:01.575
You could say:

73
00:03:01.855 --> 00:03:07.721
I made a mistake: my migration yesterday dropped an index, and that's why the reports were slow.

74
00:03:08.001 --> 00:03:10.924
I've already restored it and reports are back to normal.

75
00:03:11.204 --> 00:03:15.129
To prevent it happening again, I'm adding a migration check to CI.

76
00:03:15.409 --> 00:03:16.647
Sorry for the trouble.

77
00:03:17.997 --> 00:03:19.319
Asking for feedback.

78
00:03:19.599 --> 00:03:20.538
You could say:

79
00:03:20.818 --> 00:03:22.653
I'm working on my communication.

80
00:03:22.933 --> 00:03:25.791
What's one thing I could do differently in design reviews?

81
00:03:27.141 --> 00:03:29.019
Asking for a promotion or raise.

82
00:03:29.299 --> 00:03:30.237
You could say:

83
00:03:30.517 --> 00:03:32.203
I'd like to talk about my growth.

84
00:03:32.483 --> 00:03:39.245
Over the last year I led the checkout migration, cut deploy time by 80%, and mentored two new joiners.

85
00:03:39.525 --> 00:03:44.709
I believe I'm working at the senior level, and I'd like to be considered for promotion this cycle.

86
00:03:44.989 --> 00:03:47.869
What gaps do you see, and what would make the case clear?

87
00:03:49.219 --> 00:03:49.859
Tip.

88
00:03:50.139 --> 00:03:51.825
Bring your wins file with numbers.

89
00:03:52.105 --> 00:03:53.683
Ask for the gaps in writing.

90
00:03:53.963 --> 00:03:55.265
Agree on a timeline.

91
00:03:56.615 --> 00:03:58.129
Disagreeing with a decision.

92
00:03:58.409 --> 00:03:59.348
You could say:

93
00:03:59.628 --> 00:04:01.676
Can I share a concern before we commit?

94
00:04:01.956 --> 00:04:06.116
Building our own auth will take 6 to 8 weeks and gives us security risk.

95
00:04:06.396 --> 00:04:08.977
Keycloak gets us 90% of it in a week.

96
00:04:09.257 --> 00:04:14.420
If you still prefer custom, I'll commit to it fully, I just want to make sure we've weighed this.

97
00:04:15.770 --> 00:04:20.271
This is disagree and commit: voice it once, clearly, then support the decision.

98
00:04:21.621 --> 00:04:22.539
Part 4.

99
00:04:22.819 --> 00:04:24.120
Mistakes to avoid.

100
00:04:25.370 --> 00:04:26.223
Avoid:

101
00:04:26.503 --> 00:04:28.679
Hiding bad news until the deadline.

102
00:04:28.959 --> 00:04:29.813
Avoid:

103
00:04:30.093 --> 00:04:35.085
It's almost done for 3 weeks running (give percentages or remaining tasks instead).

104
00:04:35.365 --> 00:04:36.218
Avoid:

105
00:04:36.498 --> 00:04:39.015
Complaining about teammates without a suggestion.

106
00:04:39.295 --> 00:04:40.149
Avoid:

107
00:04:40.429 --> 00:04:43.010
Treating every one-on-one as a status report.

108
00:04:43.290 --> 00:04:44.143
Avoid:

109
00:04:44.423 --> 00:04:47.026
Waiting for your manager to bring up your career.

110
00:04:48.376 --> 00:04:49.336
Part 5.

111
00:04:49.616 --> 00:04:51.387
Beginner versus experienced.

112
00:04:52.637 --> 00:04:53.469
Beginner.

113
00:04:53.749 --> 00:04:55.285
Over-communicate status.

114
00:04:55.565 --> 00:04:57.250
Silence makes people worry.

115
00:04:57.530 --> 00:05:01.242
Write down what your manager asks so you don't have to ask twice.

116
00:05:01.522 --> 00:05:03.143
Before a one-on-one, prepare:

117
00:05:03.423 --> 00:05:05.685
1 win, 1 blocker, 1 question.

118
00:05:07.035 --> 00:05:08.123
Experienced.

119
00:05:08.403 --> 00:05:12.669
Manage up: learn your manager's goals and pressures, and help them succeed.

120
00:05:12.949 --> 00:05:15.147
Bring proposals, not just problems:

121
00:05:15.427 --> 00:05:17.603
I'd like to own the reliability roadmap.

122
00:05:17.883 --> 00:05:19.568
Give your manager feedback too:

123
00:05:19.848 --> 00:05:22.280
It would help me if decisions came with the 'why'.

124
00:05:23.630 --> 00:05:24.569
Part 6.

125
00:05:24.849 --> 00:05:26.235
Practice conversation:

126
00:05:26.515 --> 00:05:27.881
Raising a risk early.

127
00:05:29.131 --> 00:05:31.157
Here is how the conversation goes.

128
00:05:31.437 --> 00:05:33.059
Do you have five minutes?

129
00:05:33.339 --> 00:05:35.323
I want to flag a risk early.

130
00:05:35.603 --> 00:05:36.819
Sure, go ahead.

131
00:05:37.099 --> 00:05:39.275
The search feature may slip by a week.

132
00:05:39.555 --> 00:05:42.541
We need a full reindex that we didn't plan for.

133
00:05:42.821 --> 00:05:43.440
Hmm.

134
00:05:43.720 --> 00:05:45.064
What are our options?

135
00:05:46.414 --> 00:05:48.014
The conversation continues.

136
00:05:48.294 --> 00:05:53.265
Option A: we release on time without fuzzy search and add it next sprint.

137
00:05:53.545 --> 00:05:56.958
Option B: we release the full feature a week later.

138
00:05:57.238 --> 00:05:58.753
I'd recommend A.

139
00:05:59.033 --> 00:06:00.270
I agree with A.

140
00:06:00.550 --> 00:06:02.299
Can you update the product team?

141
00:06:02.579 --> 00:06:05.246
Yes, I'll message them today and copy you.

142
00:06:06.596 --> 00:06:08.132
That's the end of this lesson.

143
00:06:08.412 --> 00:06:13.063
Pick one script from it, say it out loud three times, and use it for real today.

144
00:06:13.343 --> 00:06:19.465
For extra practice, paste the Master Prompt into an AI assistant and role-play the conversation.

